<p>Ask most people what India's biggest spice export markets are, and China rarely comes up first — the Gulf and Southeast Asia dominate the popular image. The trade data says otherwise for dried red chilli specifically: China imported USD 455.8 million of it in calendar 2024, more than three times the next-largest market (Thailand, at USD 136.7 million), out of USD 1.3 billion in total Indian chilli exports worldwide. Two things shape every China-bound order: the variety and colour/heat specification you buy against, and GACC facility registration under Decree 248, which must be in place before a first shipment can clear customs. This guide covers both, plus pricing and the first-order pitfalls.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<h2>Guntur, Byadgi or Teja: choosing the variety</h2>
<p>"Red chilli" is not a usable order specification — India exports at least three genuinely different products under that heading:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Guntur (Sannam / S4)</strong> — the volume workhorse, balanced colour and moderate-to-high heat.</li>
  <li><strong>Byadgi</strong> — deep red colour with comparatively low pungency, used where colour is the objective (including oleoresin extraction).</li>
  <li><strong>Teja</strong> — very high pungency, used where heat is the primary objective.</li>
</ul>
<p>Tell us which application you're buying for and we'll recommend a variety rather than quoting against an ambiguous request.</p>

<h2>Grading by ASTA colour and Scoville heat</h2>
<p>Colour is measured in ASTA colour value; heat in Scoville Heat Units (SHU) or ASTA pungency. Beyond that, moisture and admixture percentages determine grade. Give us target values for the parameters that matter to your application and we'll quote precisely rather than against a vague "export quality" tier.</p>

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>China's Decree 248 requires facility-level registration with the General Administration of Customs of China — administered through the CIFER online system — before many food categories, chilli included, can clear customs. This is a process with real lead time, tied to the specific processing facility rather than the trading relationship alone. If you're working with a new supplier for China-bound chilli, confirming CIFER registration status is the first practical question to ask, well before discussing price or specification in detail.</p>
<p>China maintains its own residue and quality standards, not harmonised with EU or US limits. Product cleared for one market isn't automatically cleared for China. Flag China as your destination from the start of the conversation and we'll test and document against the applicable Chinese requirement.</p>
<p>Spices attract close regulatory attention across major markets, China included. We arrange pre-shipment testing on request and can provide traceability documentation linking a shipment back to its sourcing lot — useful if a quality question arises after delivery. {{ site.complianceChecklistCta }}</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<h2>What actually determines the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>We don't quote a flat per-kilogram number for "red chilli" because the variety alone changes the product entirely, before price even enters the conversation. What moves the number: variety (Teja's high-pungency positioning, Byadgi's colour-extraction premium and Guntur's volume-workhorse economics all price differently), ASTA colour grade and SHU level within a variety (tighter, higher-graded lots cost more to sort and cost more to buy), the size of that season's Andhra Pradesh and Karnataka chilli crop (chilli is a genuinely weather-sensitive crop — a poor harvest tightens supply and moves prices across every variety at once), moisture and extraneous-matter tolerances (a tighter spec means more sorting labour), and prevailing INR/USD/CNY exchange rates and freight to Chinese ports.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your specific variety, ASTA/SHU target, moisture tolerance, packaging and destination port — never as a generic market-average rate. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us: variety (Guntur, Byadgi or Teja), target ASTA colour and SHU if you have them, form (whole, stemless or crushed/ground), packaging, quantity, and destination port.</p>
